+# Privacy architecture and third-party audit
+
+Scope: the static Daily Grind website in this repository. This is a technical audit, not a claim about an external account, ordering service, or hosting provider. No browser or live deployment audit was performed.
+
+## Data inventory
+
+| Surface | Finding and protection |
+| --- | --- |
+| HTML | No forms or personal data fields; no user identifiers in markup or URLs. Privacy notice is accessible from the home page. |
+| Client scripts | None. Do not add tracking, fingerprinting, storage, permission prompts, or SDKs. CSP blocks script execution, workers, frames, forms, and programmatic connections. |
+| Browser storage | No cookies, localStorage, sessionStorage, IndexedDB, service workers, or application caches. Ordinary HTTP caching of public assets remains possible. |
+| Styles and fonts | Local stylesheet and system fonts; no font CDN, remote CSS imports, inline styles, or remote requests. |
+| Images | The original remote Unsplash background disclosed visitor connection information on page load. The same stock photo is now hosted at assets/coffee.jpg. Original stock image endpoint: https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1495474472287-4d71bcdd2085. No runtime Unsplash request. |
+| Third-party integrations | No analytics, ads, tag managers, social SDKs, embedded content, or runtime packages. No external API requests. |
+| Hosting | Provider necessarily receives connection metadata. Hosting logs, retention, TLS, and infrastructure access remain deployment responsibilities. The app adds no logging or reporting endpoint. |
+| Existing navigation | Menu, locations, rewards, account, logout, ordering, and contact routes have no implementations in this repository. These changes do not add accounts or pretend to terminate server sessions. |
+
+## Deployment
+
+Run `npm run build` (Node.js 22.16+). It runs static privacy checks and copies an explicit public-file allowlist into `dist/`. Deploy **only dist/**. No server is needed for the build.
+
+- `_headers` is included for static hosts supporting that convention (e.g. Netlify or Cloudflare Pages).
+- `vercel.json` sets the same headers and build output for Vercel.
+- Other hosts must configure these response headers at their HTTP server or CDN. The existing statikk start command is a convenience server, not a verified production header implementation.
+- Both HTML documents have an early CSP meta fallback and no-referrer meta policy. Meta CSP cannot enforce frame-ancestors; Permissions-Policy, HSTS, nosniff, COOP, and CORP also require real response headers. See https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Reference/Headers/Content-Security-Policy/frame-ancestors.
+- Serve production over HTTPS and redirect HTTP at the host. HSTS starts at 300 seconds; increase after confirming HTTPS. Do not enable includeSubDomains or preload without checking all subdomains.
+- CSP is enforced immediately because every resource in this small static site was inventoried. No scripts need nonces or hashes: script-src is none. There is no CSP reporting endpoint, avoiding collection of page URLs and code samples without a real, scrubbed reporting service.
+- Apply the policy only to this static deployment. Reassess script, connection, form, and browser-feature requirements before combining it with an ordering or payment application.
+
+## Ongoing audit and secure handling requirements
+
+`npm run check:privacy` statically checks the current markup, CSS, local resources, script-free policy, and agreement between deployment header configurations. This catches common regressions in this fixed static format; it is not a general HTML security scanner or verification of deployed response headers. Run it on every change, and repeat the inventory before adding any dependency or integration and at least quarterly. A real deployed-header/network audit remains necessary after hosting is chosen; it was not performed as part of this change.
+
+Before adding personal-data features:
+
+- Request only fields required for the immediate purpose; prefer guest ordering. Explain purpose and retention next to each field. Define deletion dates before collecting data; never repurpose security data for marketing.
+- Keep tokens and PII out of URLs, browser storage, HTML datasets, logs, and exception reports. Do not add client-side encryption with keys shipped in the same app as a substitute for safe storage.
+- Authenticate and authorize on the backend; validate input there. Use TLS, least privilege, encryption at rest, and rate limits for sensitive endpoints. Suppress request bodies, cookies, query strings, and authorization values in logs; minimize/redact IP information and set a short documented retention period at the host.
+- Session cookies must use Secure, HttpOnly, SameSite=Lax, Path=/ and preferably a __Host- name with no Domain. Protect state-changing requests against CSRF. On logout, invalidate the session on the server and return Cache-Control: no-store and Clear-Site-Data: "cookies", "storage", "cache" on that endpoint only. Do not add a global Clear-Site-Data header.
+- Provide access, correction, and deletion controls alongside any future account feature. Sensitive responses must be private/no-store; do not put account data into public static builds or caches.
+- Audit each vendor's actual data flows, purpose, retention, onward sharing, and removal mechanism. Prefer self-hosting and click-to-load facades with local thumbnails. Necessary immutable external code requires integrity hashes and anonymous CORS; review any CSP changes explicitly. Optional collection must remain off until meaningful consent and stop after withdrawal.
+
+No hosting retention duration, data-rights contact, or backend capability is invented by this implementation; establish those facts before expanding the public privacy notice.
